nd so I couldn't  ignore that and that would get me up but  then at the end of the day when I sat  with my families that was my wife my  closest friends and frankly just by  myself I was sure this was not the right  thing for me to do at this time your  wife never said things like I'll leave  you if you run for president you know  she never said that but it was clear  that this was not a choice she wished to  make what happens and I know you don't  like this scenario stay but what happens  if the nominee the Republican part  says I want you to be the vice president  I will stand on what I said at the press  conference last month I am NOT  interested in a political opportunity in  1996 and I'll stand on it the nice try  Brian should I try again no the the  thing in the in your book it's  interesting I went took your news  conference and there's a line same line  in your book and also in the news  conference about you know in the news  conference you say I did not yet have a  political eye because such a life  requires a calling that I do not yet  here and the same line is in the book  exactly did you go back and now make the  same liner ah when I when I wrote that  line in the book it was a late spring  early summer and when was the last time  that you wrote anything that is the last  word  the last comma in the book was the night  of the 3rd of July of of this year and  the neck that that night I called my  publisher and said tomorrow is July 4th  come get this thing right through 1995  1995 and the next morning somebody came  down on the on the shuttle from New York  I met her at the airport handed the  manuscript and said be gone and I never  touched it again until it was printed  and sought that was it so it was in late  spring early summer that I finished that  last chapter at last section you're  referring to the epilogue and said it  was a calling I do not get here but it  wasn't a new position for me to take I  mean I have been offered political jobs  as you noted from the book repeatedly in  the course of my military career why  don't you leave the army as an officer  and be the Undersecretary of the army  well when I was national security  advisor there was a suggestion that  perhaps I should think about politics at  that time no soldier and both parties  have approached me during the time that  I was in the army to suggest that after  the army politics may be the thing  and I never found it to be something  that connected with me because I'm  fundamentally a soldier now people call  me a political general but I'm  fundamentally a soldier that was my  ethic I had no political ambitions in  the course of my military career and I  did not feel a calling I knew who I was  as a soldier I trained all my life to  rise in the ranks of military and unique  skills are required for political life  I'm not ignorant of those skills and I'm  not ignorant of what it takes to be a  successful politician I work closely for  three presidents and I think I'm a  reasonably good politician I've had to  work with the Congress over the years  but at the point in my life when I had  to make this decision last month I did  not feel that it was the right thing for  me and I'm sure it was not the right  thing my family there's another line in  here I want to ask you because you did  change your mind on this bina this is on  page 607 because I expressed these  beliefs you're talking about  entrepreneurship and and free enterprise  system because I expressed these beliefs  some people have rushed rushed I'll be  paid to hang off Republican label around  my neck yeah they did but you decided  some point along the way hung it around  my own deck I I felt that I had seen  enough in the two years of my retirement  and I had been around the country and  these speeches that you referred to  earlier I I learned a lot of things  about what was happening in the country  a lot of people and I felt I should get  off the fence now that I'm no longer  protected by my uniform from being  partisan anyway I thought I should get  off the fence and start to actively  participate in the political life of the  country as a private citizen and I  thought I could best do that as a  Republican so I have identified myself  and I am now a member of the Republican  Party how you doing with so you want  I mean you suggested at your news  conference that she shade it we haven't  discussed this I don't know where she  will end up I'm gonna not speak for her  what about the kids but there are the  kids the kids are ok my son is a  Republican he is all  he is worked in Republican circles  previously he became a Republican before  I became a Republican what's he do now  he is a lawyer here in town fermer from  Elba Nate Meyers and he's a he's a very  fine lawyer and the two girls my two  daughters they have not told me what  their political affiliation is I'm not  sure they have decided wouldn't bet on  them being Republicans one is an actress  and the other one works at ABC Nightline  she only works Anne Marie Ann Marie  works for Ted Koppel and Nightline and  Linda is the other Linda's a very very  accomplished actress she's appearing in  Baltimore now and she'll be coming to  Arena Stage here in Washington shortly  speaking of acting in this audiotape  there were moments where you were a  little more dramatic in others and I  want to play one for you this is a story  you've told before let's listen to this  and get your reaction to this part of  the tape on a speech circuit I tell a  story that goes to the heart of  America's longing the ABC correspondent  Sam Donaldson was interviewing a young  african-american soldier in a tank  platoon on the eve of battle and Desert  Storm Donaldson asked how do you think  the battle will go  are you afraid we will do okay we're  well trained and I'm not afraid the G I  answered gesturing toward his buddies  around him  I'm not afraid because I'm with my  family the other soldiers shouted tell  him again he didn't hear you  souls are repeated this is my family and  we'll take care of each other that story  never fails to touch me or the audience  it is a metaphor for what we have to do  as a nation we have to start thinking of  America as a family we have to stop  screeching at each other stop hurting  each other and instead start caring for  sacrificing for and sharing with each  other there's more what do you think of  the drama there well it's it's my  favorite story I've told us hundreds of  times  and I use it in my my speech around the  country and always touched by it's a  wonderful piece of television that Sam  Donaldson did on the audiotape I gussied  it up a little bit but the audiotape was  designed to be somebody sitting in a car  with you talking to you you're not  supposed to be watching a mini drama  just remember that you're driving along  in a car with somebody telling him this  story but look back on that on the whole  book to earth all the interviews what do  you think of the xperia what would you  tell somebody that was about to do this  what did you learn from it I found it  very very enjoyable but very very  exhausting I had talked to other authors  out  I would tell folks that one of the sad  things about it is if you're expecting a  large crowd only thing you can do is  your signature  regrettably you can't say to aunt Minnie  and uncle Ezra I would say make  eye-contact and show some human response  to everybody who goes through the line I  would say make sure you well-rested and  enjoy the experience it I I don't know  most authors do not go out for five  weeks it's usually a much shorter tour  and it's it's seldom as many as 25  cities but it can be done and I had a  good time on it I also went to two  countries during that five-week period I  went to England I went to France what  about the media part of it the  interviews the interviews I found those  to be very interesting and the what I  haven't done a lot of is radio and on  this tour I got a chance to do a lot of  radio and I found that to be very very  interesting and enjoyable because you  usually had enough time to develop a  theme on a radio and it isn't it isn't  quite as demanding as television you  didn't have to be as OnPoint as I think  you have to be on television and so I  got a lot of radio experience but I  found it most enjoyable on the cover of  this book is your photo as taken by  Annie Leibovitz famous photographer name  is pathar  what do you think of that idea Annie has  photographed me before she did a Vanity  Fair shot of me right after the Gulf War  so I was pleased that she was available  to to do this shot working with her is  great because she is an absolute  professional we did it in a in a library  here in Washington she took lots of  shots of me in uniform and civilian  clothes we even have a Larry King in  suspenders kind of shot but of all the  shots that's the one that we selected  that's the one I published we thought it  was best  this seems out of context but in the  book you referred to reading street  without joy by Barnard fall Frenchman  died back during the Vietnam War as  quickly as well why'd you read that and  what impact today at that time in the  early 60s when we were trying to learn  about Vietnam Barnard fall was  considered one of the great experts on  the country because he had Chronicle the  French experience in French Indochina in  Vietnam and so we all read Barnard fall  as part of our training streak without  joy was the story of route 1 the main  artery going up South Vietnam that the  French had such difficulty along and we  probably all should have studied Bernard  fall a lot longer and was greater  intensity especially people in high  policy positions because he made it  clear in that book and we should have  realized that that it was war as much  about nationalism and self-determination  within this one country than it was  about the ideology of communism or the  worldwide communist conspiracy reading I  bring it up is the what I want to ask  you the general question about books how  often have books have had an impact and  you mention a lot of books in there yeah  I read a great deal I haven't read as  many books as I would like to in recent  years because when you chairman of the  Joint Chiefs of Staff you're reading  things from 7 o'clock in the morning  when the intelligence folks show up  until late that night and it's difficult  to get into novels or into biographies  or historical books but them I'm getting  back in the swing of things now  reading a few things that are strictly  for fun and for my general rounding as  opposed to work when you in the book you  say and we've talked about this in other  interviews about your that you call it a  turning point in your life when you were  selected for the White House Fellows  program why do you think you were  selected and why do you think is a  turning point I think I was selected  because I was doing rather well in my  career as a military officer  it's a very intense interview process  you have to go through go through and I  think I interviewed well with the people  who were who were selecting fellows that  year I was a little more mature than the  other fellows it was it was an age limit  at that time before it became  discriminatory to have age limits and I  was at the upper edge of the age limit  and the people who were doing the  panel's that year saw something in me  that they found attractive and thought  it useful to pick me and give me this  experience it was a turning point  because until then I was just your  average army officer going along through  life learning how to be a better  infantry officer and suddenly I went  from being just your average army  officer to somebody who was working in  the White House meeting presidents  working in the Office of Management and  Budget examining the whole federal  government getting exposed to the whole  federal government seeing how Congress  works seeing how policy is being made at  the highest levels of of the government  and then after a year of this suddenly  I'm back to being an infantry officer I  was shipped off to Korea to command a  battalion but that experience of seeing  government at that level stayed with me  so that about five or six years later  when I am now a brigadier general the  gentleman who I had met in my White  House fellows year at OMB cap Weinberger  and Frank Carlucci come back into my  life remember me and I go to work for  them and was able to use the high-level  government experiences that I had had  years earlier as White House Fellow so  it really fundamentally changed the  shape of my career in the shape of my  life after you discuss in the book a lot  of Republicans and the  the Weinberger's in people like that you  do make this statement at the end that  you and President Clinton were close  would you mean by that we got along very  very well we obviously had differences  on some issues gays and lesbians in the  military but as a personal matter we got  along very very well we do to this day  even though I have I have gone on to  become a Republican we talking the phone  from time to time and we have very very  cordial discussions and I like President  Clinton and one we wouldn't be first  started working together at the  beginning of his administration and we  had this very difficult issue of whether  or not to allow gays and lesbians in the  military and I said to him after one of  the meetings first meeting on the  subject I said mr. president I'm very  very sorry that this had to be the first  issue that you had to deal with what the  military I hoped it would be something  else and I regret that this is the case  and what he said to me was I understand  that Colin and but you know this is a  very tough issue it was easy somebody  would have solved it before we came  along so we will just work this problem  and that that showed understanding for  the problem that those of us in the  military had with this issue at that  time and still do and we he and I were  able to work every issue that we faced  in that in that same way problems to be  dealt with I spent a lot of quiet  private time with President Clinton  usually when I went to see him we would  meet alone one on one with no one else  for long periods of time so I think I  have a good relationship with President  Clinton even though I'm sure we will  have areas of disagreement in the future  as we've had in the past on various  things I'm going to try one more time on  something I know you're going to smile  up what is your hunch about your future  you think it think you'll ever find  yourself out there shaking hands saying  vote for me anytime I can't I can't  answer that  and I won't I won't try to one of the  things I did learn along the way is  don't  trapped into trying to answer  hypothetical questions about the future  the beauty of my personal situation and  and the wonderful thing about the  country we live in is that there are all  kinds of opportunities out there there  are all sorts of doors that are that are  waiting to be open I would hope that my  my future life will be one of service to  society in one way or another I'm not  sure how best to perform that service is  there anything about the Republic being  a Republican that you don't like I have  I have said candidly that I think there  are some aspects of a Republican  political activity right now that are  they're a little too harsh I think we're  a Republican Party as a party is coming  down a little too harshly on affirmative  action it isn't all bad it isn't all  preferences and quotas very very good  things have happened with good  affirmative action over the last thirty  years and I think we have to be careful  as we get rid of things that no longer  make sense like quotas and and  preferences that are no longer based on  anything we can't show such a harshness  to the affirmative action that has  produced such good things over the years  that we communicate the wrong signal to  people who are still in need of  affirmative action and I've criticized  my party for that I also think we have  to be very very careful as we change the  social safety net system as we change  welfare reform and Medicaid and Medicare  things that do need change and the  Democrats are going to change as well we  have to remember that at the at the far  end of this process there are there are  young people and in many cases there are  young people who have only one parent at  home who are in need and if we don't  make sure that they are provided for in  some way that we better start building  all the jails we're going to need for  them in 18 years when when the call  comes from the people running for the  sent in the house for you to come speak  for them you're going to do it some of  the calls have started to come in  already at the moment I have elected not  to do any active campaigning or  political fundraising I am a private  citizen who happens to be a Republican  and here is the book of the private  citizen called  my American Journey Colin Powell with  Joseph II Persico thank you for joining  us thank you very much Brian
 

